  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Energy is the morning's story again. Brent is up 1.1% near $85 and WTI +0.8% after a vessel reported being struck by an "unknown projectile" in the Strait of Hormuz, while Trump threatened fresh air strikes against Iran and framed his latest offer of talks as Tehran's "last chance," demanding a full reopening of the waterway. Tehran denies negotiating with Washington, though talks via Oman on shipping flows are reportedly progressing — leaving the Hormuz risk premium firmly embedded. European gas caught the same bid, TTF jumping 4.3%, and EUAs firmed 1.5%, though products lagged with gasoil down sharply.

Emerging-market assets stayed under pressure as the Iran impasse keeps energy costs elevated, and the diesel pass-through to US consumers is already visible according to reports. The rest of the tape is constructive despite the geopolitics. Equity-index futures are higher across the board — Nasdaq +0.8%, Dow +0.5% after Monday's record close — helped by a heavy, broadly strong earnings slate, though headlines that Washington is drafting a ban on China data-center components keep the tech-trade fault line live.

Rates tell the more durable macro story: the long end is softly offered again (long bond -0.14%, gilts -0.36%) as post-FOMC term-premium repricing continues, with AI capex and persistent government borrowing competing for capital in a supply-constrained world — a structural headwind for duration futures and a reason curve steepeners keep working.

